  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "L1 bromfenac, a close benzoyl-aryl-acetic-acid analog of the query, has measured absolute oral bioavailability of 67 ± 20% in fasting healthy subjects after a 50 mg oral dose versus IV, directly supporting F ≥ 20% for this acidic-NSAID chemotype.",
      "card_ids": ["C05", "C10", "C18", "C23"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 records for ketoprofen, the closest benzoyl-aryl-propanoic-acid analog, report human fraction absorbed of 90-100%, rapid/almost complete absorption, Caco-2/HT29-MTX permeability of 4.19-4.78 ×10^-5 cm/s, and solubility of 0.49 mg/mL at pH 4.6, indicating efficient intestinal absorption despite acidic ionization.",
      "card_ids": ["C92", "C101", "C133", "C96", "C124", "C127", "C113", "C106", "C104", "C117", "C103", "C100", "C116"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 records for tolmetin (F > 0.90) and tiaprofenic acid (total absorption in all species), both benzoyl/heteroaryl carboxylic acids, further support high absorption for the query's benzoyl-pyrrolizine-carboxylic acid.",
      "card_ids": ["C114", "C132"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 records show carrier-mediated intestinal uptake of salicylic acid via monocarboxylic acid transporters and bidirectional Caco-2 transport of aspirin, with no L5 record providing affirmative efflux or intestinal-metabolism evidence for F < 20% in this acidic-NSAID chemotype.",
      "card_ids": ["C146", "C137", "C138"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral exposure records for ketoprofen show measurable plasma AUC and Cmax after oral dosing, consistent with absorption, though they lack matched IV comparators and are not used as direct F evidence.",
      "card_ids": ["C65", "C61", "C75", "C78"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C146", "C137", "C138", "C155"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct ketorolac-specific F, AUC, Cmax, permeability, solubility, efflux, or intestinal-metabolism record is supplied; quantitative support is transferred from close analogs such as bromfenac, ketoprofen, tolmetin, and tiaprofenic acid.",
    "L3 exposure records lack matched IV comparators, so absolute F cannot be derived from them alone.",
    "L5 contains no ketorolac-specific gut-wall efflux or intestinal-metabolism measurement; the supportive records are for salicylic acid, aspirin, and p-coumaric acid and are mechanistically transferable but not direct.",
    "The query's physicochemical prior is unfavorable for passive transcellular absorption (neutral fraction 0, logD -0.86), but experimental analog data indicate that ionized acidic NSAIDs can be well absorbed; residual uncertainty about transporter/efflux behavior remains."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains high and is kept at high confidence: L5 adds no affirmative low-F evidence and provides some mechanistic support for intestinal uptake of acidic compounds (salicylic acid monocarboxylic-acid transporter, aspirin Caco-2 transport). Combined with L1 bromfenac F=67% and L4 ketoprofen/tolmetin/tiaprofenic acid absorption records, the weight of evidence supports F ≥ 20%."
}

